22:22:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: During the warmer months, glaciers lose mass primarily through surface melting, and most of this meltwater flows away as runoff. In the cooler months, glaciers do regain mass, but **not because the summer meltwater itself refreezes back into the glacier in large quantities.** Instead, the recovery comes from fresh snowfall accumulating on the glacier’s surface, which over time compacts into new ice. Here’s a more detailed breakdown: - **Summer melt vs. winter accumulation** A glacier’s health depends on the balance between **accumulation** (mass gained, mainly from snow) and **ablation** (mass lost, mainly from melting). - In many alpine glaciers, 70–90% or more of the winter snowfall can melt during a warm summer, and only a fraction of that meltwater refreezes within the glacier. - During cooler months, new snow adds mass, but whether it offsets summer loss determines if the glacier grows, shrinks, or stays balanced. - **Refreezing of meltwater (“superimposed ice” and internal accumulation)** Some summer meltwater does refreeze, but usually in limited amounts: – Meltwater percolating into cold snow or firn can refreeze, forming layers of **superimposed ice** or releasing latent heat. – In very cold environments (e.g., high polar regions), this refreezing can be more significant, but globally it’s a **small percentage** of the total summer melt. – Most meltwater runs off into streams and doesn’t return to the glacier. - **The bigger picture: net mass balance** If winter snowfall is sufficient to replace the summer’s melted mass, the glacier has a **positive net balance** and advances. If not, the net balance is negative and the glacier retreats. With climate warming, many glaciers worldwide are losing more mass in summer than they gain in winter, leading to overall shrinkage—and the amount that “freezes back” from summer melt isn’t nearly enough to cancel the loss. So, in short: **very little of the summer meltwater gets f