22:22:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a great analogy question — and it gets right to the heart of why glaciers behave so differently from a simple ice cube tray. The key difference is: **in your freezer, the melted water stays right where it melted, so when it gets cold again, that same water can refreeze in place.** A glacier does not work that way. Here’s why your freezer analogy breaks down for a glacier: ### 1. The meltwater doesn’t hang around When ice melts in your freezer tray, the water pools in the tray. Cool the freezer, and that same water turns back into ice — the mass is conserved. When a glacier’s surface melts, the water **flows downhill and off the glacier** through streams, crevasses, and moulins (vertical shafts). Once it leaves the glacier system, it’s gone forever — into rivers and oceans. It never gets a chance to just sit there and refreeze when winter returns. ### 2. Glaciers gain mass from new snowfall, not from recycled meltwater The recovery in winter comes from fresh snow falling on top, which slowly compacts into ice over years. That’s totally different from refreezing the summer melt. In your freezer, the “refill” is literally the same water refreezing; for a glacier, the refill is new material added from above. ### 3. Even when meltwater does refreeze inside the glacier, it’s a tiny fraction Some summer meltwater does percolate down into cold snow or firn (dense, old snow) and refreezes there — that’s called **internal accumulation**. This can form “superimposed ice” layers. But: - This only works if the glacier’s interior is still below freezing, so the percolating water doesn’t just drain away. - The amount that actually refreezes is usually a small fraction of total melt (often less than 10–20% even in cold polar settings). Most of the water still runs off. ### 4. Physics of scale and time Your freezer is a tiny closed system where temperature changes quickly and water is trapped. A glacier is a massive, open system with constant drain