hat doesn’t replace the massive summer loss. Documentaries love this time-lapse because it’s a visible “healing” of the glacier. **3. Frozen meltwater channels and icicles** In autumn, as temperatures drop, the meltwater streams still trickling on the glacier’s surface freeze solid. Deep crevasses that were gushing with water become huge ice-clogged shafts. Overhanging seracs drip melt that freezes into giant icicles. All of this footage screams “the glacier is refreezing,” and it is—but the water that already left the glacier system months ago is gone. This frozen-in-place water was just the last dregs that hadn’t escaped yet. ### So why does this not contradict the “most meltwater is lost” fact? Think of it this way: imagine a giant block of ice with a small dent that catches a puddle of water, while most of the meltwater cascades off into a drain. When you freeze the whole setup in winter, the puddle in the dent does freeze, adding a tiny skin of ice back to the surface. Your camera could zoom in on that puddle refreezing and make a compelling clip. But the 95% of water that went down the drain is still gone. That’s what a glacier experiences: the internal and superficial refreezing is the puddle in the dent; the vast majority is the drain. Even in the most optimistic cases (like very cold polar glaciers), the proportion of summer melt that refreezes within the glacier is typically under 20-30%, and often far less in temperate alpine glaciers. The rest runs off. The winter’s snowfall is the real rebuild—not the refreezing of lost meltwater. ### The school lesson and the documentary: both contain a grain of truth Your school’s “cycle” diagram showed accumulation in winter and ablation in summer, which is a cycle of *mass*. The documentary showed the dramatic refreezing that actually can happen *on the glacier surface* under the right conditions, which makes for stunning television. Neither is entirely wrong; they just zoom in on different scales. The full picture 