22:24:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a completely logical question—and it hits on exactly the reason this can feel so counterintuitive. Let me address that directly. You're thinking of the glacier as a closed system, like a puddle: melt water, then freeze it again, same water stays. If that were the case, then yes, the glacier must refreeze its own water to survive. But a glacier is **not a closed system**—it's an **open system** with a steady income and a separate expense. The "rebuilding" doesn't come from recycling the same meltwater that left; it comes from **new material added from the outside**. ### The glacier’s annual "paycheck": snowfall Every winter (and often year-round at high elevations), snow falls on the glacier. That snow accumulates layer by layer, compresses into firn, and eventually becomes new glacier ice. That's the **income**—brand-new water mass delivered from the atmosphere. It has nothing to do with the meltwater that drained away months earlier. The **expense** is summer melt running off the glacier and into rivers. So the equation is simple: - If snowfall (income) ≥ melt (expense), the glacier survives or grows. - If melt > snowfall, the glacier shrinks. When a healthy glacier doesn't vanish, it's because its *winter snow income* was enough to replace the summer losses—not because the summer meltwater magically returned. ### Why your "near zero refreeze" scenario would indeed mean death—if there were no snowfall You're absolutely right: **if a glacier had no new snow and relied entirely on refreezing its own meltwater, it would rapidly disappear**, because most of that meltwater flows away. In fact, that's exactly what's happening to many glaciers today. Warmer temperatures mean more melting and less snowfall, so the net balance is negative. The tiny bit of refreezing that *does* occur is nowhere near enough to save them. They are vanishing, just as your logic predicts—because the income (snow) is dropping while the expense (melt) is rising. ### The "cycle" you learned about is a cycle of *mass*, not of the same water molecules Think of a glacier like a bank account. - **Deposits** = winter snow. - **Withdrawals** = summer meltwater that leaves the glacier. - You can keep the account balance steady if deposits equal withdrawals, even though the *exact same dollar bills* you withdrew never return to your account. New money comes in from your salary. - A glacier's salary is snowfall. The meltwater is spent money, gone to the ocean. No single water molecule needs to return to the glacier for the *mass* to cycle annually. The cycle is: water leaves, new water arrives as snow. ### But wait—doesn't *some* meltwater refreeze and help? Yes, as we discussed, a small fraction of meltwater refreezes within cold snow or as superimposed ice before it can run off. That's like finding a few coins in your pocket and putting them back in the account—it's real but a minor contribution. The bulk of the rebuilding is snow. So, to directly answer your statement: glaciers don't vanish every year because they are **rebuilt by fresh snowfall**, not by the refreezing of meltwater that leaked away. Your instinct that they'd be doomed if they depended on refreezing is exactly right—and that's precisely why snowfall is the lifeline. Without it, they'd be gone in a few years.
